  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole didn't give them much to work with, so the offense found a way through one clean inning.",
    "what_this_means": "Gerrit Cole shut out Chicago over six innings. Four hits, zero runs, seven strikeouts. And that should have been enough. It wasn't, not quite. The Yankees scored three in the second, took the lead and held it, then watched the White Sox chip away late. David Bednar closed it out in the ninth with two outs to spare, but not before the margin collapsed to one. This is what Cole's line buys you: control and precision. It does not buy you room for error. The team converted two of eight runners in scoring position and that was the difference between a comfortable win and a one-run hold. Cole did his job. The offense did enough, but not more.",
    "what_to_watch": "They are now two games in on a winning streak, 1.5 back in the division, and they got here by doing the minimum. That's not sustainable. The bullpen. Chivilli, Headrick, Bednar. Held three scoreless innings after Cole left, which matters more when the starter leaves early than when he pitches deep. This game felt smaller than it was. A shutout from the ace and an early lead should feel like a platform. Instead it felt like a narrow escape. They wasted two runners on base in the sixth. They didn't get another chance. The White Sox scored two in the ninth and made this close. This is the cost of leaving runners on base. Not in philosophy, but in actual run differential. Good teams don't let nights like this stack.",
